WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.600
More than two months into the war in Ukraine&nbsp;
and both sides are taking heavy losses. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:12.160
the bombardment goes on, Moscow has kept up its&nbsp;
relentless propaganda, accusing NATO of conducting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:18.560
a proxy war with Russia. My guest this week is&nbsp;
Lesia Vasylenko, politician and human rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:26.000
lawyer, who joins me from Kyiv. What makes her so&nbsp;
sure Mr. Putin wouldn’t press his nuclear button?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.240
“In all honesty Putin has made so many&nbsp;
claims which he never went ahead with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.280
Saying that he is willing to&nbsp;
deploy nuclear weapons in Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.000
would be suicidal to him and to his own&nbsp;
country and his own people as well.”

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.040
In recent weeks she has had&nbsp;
blunt words for the West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.560
and NATO – accusing the alliance of ‘cowardice’&nbsp;
and ‘watching the war as if it were a movie’.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.680
Does she still feel that way? And what&nbsp;
about a future peace deal with Russia?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.920
Is there any sign at all of what it might look&nbsp;
like? All that and much more on Conflict Zone

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.680
Lesia Vasylenko, welcome to Conflict Zone.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.440
Thank you. It's a pleasure to be here.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.400
More than two months into this war, some 5 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:24.800
of your people have left as refugees. But now&nbsp;
hundreds of thousands are coming back, sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:31.040
against the advice of local authorities. What does&nbsp;
that tell you about your people, your country?

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.720
It's a resilient and stubborn people that&nbsp;
Russia has to deal with. And we are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.840
confident in our victory and very confident&nbsp;
in the victory of freedom and independence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.240
And the people who you are seeing&nbsp;
coming back are the people who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.000
more than anything, want to live&nbsp;
in a free and independent Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.360
and are prepared to do everything to rebuild&nbsp;
it from the wrath of Russia's aggression.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.520
We've seen Ukrainians forced&nbsp;
to confront unbelievable pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.800
and loss almost on a daily basis. Some&nbsp;
have spoken in interviews about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:11.520
continuing disbelief at what's happening&nbsp;
to them. Do you share that feeling?

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:19.840
Every single Ukrainian has lost something in&nbsp;
this war. The worst is people having to endure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:28.480
losses of close ones like family members and dear&nbsp;
friends. People have lost property, have lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:37.120
houses, homes. Everyone's at a loss. Why it&nbsp;
happened? Because of Russia. We know it very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:44.720
clearly now. How it was possible to even go as&nbsp;
far as the Russians did with all the crimes they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.880
are committing currently in the territory&nbsp;
of Ukraine. This is something unfathomable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.800
This is something that many Ukrainians,&nbsp;
myself included, still cannot puzzle together.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.480
What have you learned about&nbsp;
yourself during this period?

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.960
A lot. I think that myself, like many other&nbsp;
Ukrainian women, we uncover the strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:13.200
that we never knew we had before. These past&nbsp;
two months and a bit have been about unity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.840
and have been about resilience for every single&nbsp;
person, for every single family in Ukraine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:26.400
We have discovered all, myself included,&nbsp;
that we are a strong people. We are a nation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:34.160
a united nation, and a nation that really, really&nbsp;
loves its country and is prepared to die for it.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.720
And the knowledge, as you say, prepared to die&nbsp;
for it. But the knowledge that life could end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.840
today, tonight, tomorrow for you and&nbsp;
those who you care about. Is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:51.600
an ever present feeling? And does that sap&nbsp;
your focus or does it in a way inspire you?

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.440
I've learned to live with it personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:01.280
and so have millions of Ukrainians who remained&nbsp;
in Ukraine. It's a strange feeling that, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:09.200
that it can all end tomorrow. But I think the&nbsp;
fear of losing freedom and losing life in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.720
free Ukraine, losing the home is greater&nbsp;
than the fear of losing your own life.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.600
You mentioned the crimes that are being&nbsp;
committed and you've been keeping track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.400
of some of the war crimes allegations that&nbsp;
have been piling up against Russian forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:31.120
especially when it comes to the treatment of women&nbsp;
and girls. How shocking has that been for you?

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.200
That is exactly what my mind cannot put&nbsp;
together. The puzzle pieces are there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:43.680
right in front of me, on the table, with the facts&nbsp;
of the rapes committed against Ukrainian women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:50.880
Ukrainian children, Ukrainian unarmed men. I&nbsp;
have the facts of numerous tortures. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.560
all there. Laid out on the table sometimes with&nbsp;
absolutely devastating pictures to go with them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:05.280
But my brain cannot process the cruelty&nbsp;
behind it, the intent and the violence. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.000
this is the restlessness with&nbsp;
which I also had to learn to live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.640
with the restlessness of the brain, which&nbsp;
then leads to many sleepless nights.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.120
Do you believe that Russian commanders&nbsp;
have ordered these crimes that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.560
alleged to have been committed or&nbsp;
simply turned a blind eye to them?

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.240
I believe that it's a strategy of the Russian&nbsp;
army as well as of the leadership of Russia as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:34.480
a country to destroy Ukraine and Ukrainians in&nbsp;
every way possible. What the Russians are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:40.800
is more than just physically killing Ukrainian&nbsp;
soldiers. They are trying to erase the Ukrainian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.440
nation from the face of the earth and they&nbsp;
are doing this in so many different ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.760
The fact that our children&nbsp;
today, for more than two months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:56.080
have been deprived of proper education, the&nbsp;
schools are closed, the kindergartens are closed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:02.160
the universities are closed all across Ukraine,&nbsp;
there's only online schooling. The children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.560
are not in schools learning new things,&nbsp;
not socializing with their kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.920
but they are hiding in bomb shelters. They are in&nbsp;
the basements, hiding from Russian artillery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.840
from Russian soldiers who are hunting them down to&nbsp;
kill them, to torture them, to rape them. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:26.560
is a future generation of Ukrainians which will&nbsp;
be growing up with huge traumas and with very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:34.320
very limited education and learning skills. This&nbsp;
is Ukraine we are talking about: a European nation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:41.280
which has prided itself for years in the number&nbsp;
of literate people, literate children, of the

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.040
successes, educational successes,&nbsp;
of the children in schools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:49.040
And Russia is taking that away. They&nbsp;
are destroying that deliberately.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:52.800
What about the chances of bringing&nbsp;
winnable prosecutions against any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.520
the soldiers that have been committing&nbsp;
these acts? So far I believe ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:04.080
have been identified as suspects from the unit&nbsp;
that occupied Bucha, where the bodies of civilians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:10.960
apparently shot in summary executions were left&nbsp;
lying in the streets. Do you have any hope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.920
these people might be brought to&nbsp;
trial, might be brought to justice?

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:21.760
I'm confident that justice will be served, will&nbsp;
be served in the Ukrainian courts and within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:28.560
Ukrainian justice system. It will be served&nbsp;
in international judicial bodies such as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:35.040
International Criminal Court, International Court&nbsp;
of Justice. And I firmly believe that eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.480
the world will set up a special criminal&nbsp;
tribunal for Putin, all his cronies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.640
all the generals and all the soldiers who&nbsp;
have been committing war crimes in Ukraine.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:50.160
Talk to me, if you will, about life under&nbsp;
occupation in Kherson, for instance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.000
Is there anything in Russia's behavior&nbsp;
there that allows you to form any view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:02.080
of their long-term aims, the Russians long-term&nbsp;
aims. I gather the mayor has been replaced. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:07.200
currency is being replaced with Russian rubles.&nbsp;
Reports that Russian textbooks have been brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:12.480
into schools, that the Internet has been&nbsp;
disconnected and reconnected to Moscow's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:16.720
own system. Is this the template, as&nbsp;
far as you're concerned, preparations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.520
being made for permanent occupation of&nbsp;
the places where they are at the moment?

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.960
It looks like it. I mean, you asked me a couple of&nbsp;
questions back about whether there's a fear about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:33.200
dying. And I said to you that there's a fear about&nbsp;
losing this freedom. You can rephrase it by saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:40.000
that it’s a fear of living under such conditions&nbsp;
that you have just described of occupying Kherson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:46.480
where you just don't lose everything, like&nbsp;
physically because your home is destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:54.000
but you are told how to live. You are being&nbsp;
deprived of information channels. You are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.520
being deprived of the language, of your history,&nbsp;
of your currency, even. And you are being told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.320
that this is it, this is how you live now:&nbsp;
you have no choice. And I think this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:13.680
worst fear that every Ukrainian has. And for us,&nbsp;
Kherson, Melitopol, Mariupol, they are all cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:19.280
which epitomize what the Russians are trying&nbsp;
to impose on all of Ukraine. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:25.360
this gets the Ukrainian nation even more united&nbsp;
and even more determined to fight even harder.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:30.000
What's the role of a of a politician like&nbsp;
you under these circumstances? What can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.840
you do for your constituents? How do you&nbsp;
even keep track of the living and the dead?

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:42.480
Today it's much easier than back in the&nbsp;
day, I imagine, because of the social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:50.240
and because everybody literally has devices&nbsp;
with Internet and can do a wider outreach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.600
And this is how we learn today of the needs of the&nbsp;
people. The role of politicians is to be here on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:01.200
the ground with the people of Ukraine, supporting&nbsp;
them, liaising them with the partners, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:06.720
international partners who can provide assistance&nbsp;
and who can provide relief to the various needs…

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:11.600
And we're talking about basic needs here.&nbsp;
We're talking about getting food to people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.520
medicine, care for the sick and&nbsp;
elderly, that sort of thing.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:18.560
Humanitarian aid. Absolutely.&nbsp;
If you want one word for it.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:22.480
How and at what level do&nbsp;
the mechanics of daily life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:25.920
continue, though, under this constant bombardment?

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.400
Actually now it has become more or&nbsp;
less back to normal, if you like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:36.160
I'm talking now from downtown Kyiv. I see in&nbsp;
the street to a lot of cars, a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:41.200
passing by. The shops are reopened. The&nbsp;
cafes are reopened. Nevertheless, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.440
air raid sirens sounding from time to time&nbsp;
and we learn to live with it in the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:57.280
and learn to make the best of it. And every&nbsp;
single person has found a space in their life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:03.120
for volunteer work, for helping others who are&nbsp;
worse off than they are. And the same goes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:08.160
parliamentarians, politicians, for government&nbsp;
officials. Apart from our regular duties about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:13.520
writing laws, debating them in parliament, voting&nbsp;
on them, discussing them in committees, we find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:20.480
a lot of space and a lot of time for coordinating&nbsp;
volunteer work, for coordinating humanitarian aid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:26.880
for coordinating military assistance to the&nbsp;
different territorial defense units and to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:33.280
different military units even. So, everybody's&nbsp;
doing their bit to keep Ukraine safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:37.440
to keep the community safe, and at the end of the&nbsp;
day, to keep their families and themselves safe.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:45.040
You said recently that 80% of your thoughts&nbsp;
are directed at the now, the war, but 20%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:50.720
is thinking about the future and about&nbsp;
reconstruction. How vital is that from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:55.840
a practical point of view and I suppose&nbsp;
from the point of view of morale as well?

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:03.280
It's absolutely important to look to the future,&nbsp;
to know your past and to act in the present. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:08.160
of course, the conditions are such today that we&nbsp;
have to concentrate on the present to survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:14.800
and that's the main thing. But then again, we're&nbsp;
talking about people coming back to Ukraine. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:19.920
talking about people coming back to find&nbsp;
destruction in the towns that have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:25.040
occupied by Russia and have been freed by&nbsp;
Ukraine. And these towns need rebuilding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.960
This is just a simple example as that.&nbsp;
And to rebuild them, there needs to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:34.080
vision of rebuild. There needs to be a plan&nbsp;
like there was a Marshall Plan for Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:38.400
there must be a certain plan for Ukraine&nbsp;
today. We call it the Brussels plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:44.240
because essentially it's the EU institutions&nbsp;
which have come forward and which have started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:49.840
to devise, devise a certain scheme as to how&nbsp;
to renew Ukraine and how much money needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:56.000
be allocated. The UK, although already not part of&nbsp;
the EU, but the UK is playing a big role as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:02.160
There's a whole team of British economists which&nbsp;
is now working with Ukrainian specialists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:08.480
experts to also devise a plan of economic recovery&nbsp;
which would touch also on the energy sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:15.360
on the agricultural sector, on the industrial&nbsp;
and trading sectors. So work is being done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:22.000
But of course the Ukrainian counterparts&nbsp;
cannot dedicate 100% of time to that work.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:28.240
Mrs. Vasylenko, on the political front,&nbsp;
you've recently been very critical of the UN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:35.280
Secretary-General Antonio Guterres because he went&nbsp;
to Moscow a few days ago before going to Kyiv.

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:40.160
What did he bring back from Russia? Anything that&nbsp;
your government thought was worth listening to?

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:48.880
He brought back a missile attack on a building&nbsp;
in Kyiv which was destroyed and several people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:56.160
died from that missile attack and ten or so were&nbsp;
injured and taken to hospital with heavy wounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:01.840
Apart from that, not much has resulted.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:04.480
Not really his fault, though.

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:12.960
Not. Not really his fault. No. But, you know, the&nbsp;
point is that I think, this is the point that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:20.640
tried to make many, many times, is that the UN&nbsp;
needs reform. The UN as a system has failed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:26.080
has failed big time to prevent wars in the past&nbsp;
and to prevent this war, Russia's aggression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:30.960
which has been going on for eight years&nbsp;
now. Russia staying on the Security Council,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:37.040
having veto power, is absolute nonsense. I mean,&nbsp;
Russia is responsible for the insecurity in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:42.160
world and the instability in the world today.&nbsp;
And they are there, making security decisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:49.840
We have, as Ukrainians, time and time again raised&nbsp;
the issue about the reform of the UN system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:57.520
We were not heard. Then when we have the Secretary&nbsp;
General first going to Moscow and then coming to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:05.200
Kyiv, that also doesn't make sense. Why go and pay&nbsp;
the respects and the dues to the aggressor first?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:11.120
Come, come to Ukraine, see what Russia has done,&nbsp;
and then go to Putin with the demands, not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:17.600
other way round. This was the point I was trying&nbsp;
to make. But ok, to be fair, to be fair, after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:26.320
secretary general's visit, the evacuation of the&nbsp;
people from Azovstal, the steel works in Mariupol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:35.040
has started and has had many successes. So I'm&nbsp;
grateful for that. I would love to believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:41.440
it's because there were these negotiations with&nbsp;
the UN Secretary General, and if that is the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:47.040
then with great gratitude and great respect,&nbsp;
I say thank you to the UN Secretary General.

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:53.440
Along with other Ukrainian MPs you've spent&nbsp;
some time shuttling between Western capitals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:58.560
explaining Ukraine's position, its requirements,&nbsp;
its views, and especially its suffering. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:05.040
you've heard a lot of overarching expressions&nbsp;
of solidarity and promises of full support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:08.080
but you haven't been that impressed&nbsp;
with them, have you? A lot of them.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:16.000
Not with all of them. Words are words and&nbsp;
words are important. The solidarity messages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:22.080
they are well received here in Ukraine. They&nbsp;
also allowed the Ukrainian people to go on for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:27.760
so long because knowing that there's this --and&nbsp;
feeling this immense support from every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:33.840
country in Europe, almost every single country in&nbsp;
Europe on the continent, was a great push for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:40.000
Ukrainians to keep going. To know that we're not&nbsp;
alone in this. And to know that the big European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:47.760
family to which we strive so much, is there with&nbsp;
us. That's important. But of course, what I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:54.640
like to see more of is that these words would be&nbsp;
backed up by action, and we are slowly getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.640 --> 00:17:01.680
there. So Ukraine is getting weapons and Ukraine&nbsp;
is getting more help in terms of humanitarian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:06.640
financial assistance, and Russia is getting more&nbsp;
and more sanctions around it. And these sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:13.360
are becoming more robust. The only thing is that&nbsp;
it would have been better for everyone’s sake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:17.840
not just for Ukraine, if all of this was&nbsp;
happening faster and a couple of months back.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:23.600
But then time a few weeks ago, you accused&nbsp;
Western countries of doing the bare minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:29.600
and standing on the sidelines watching Ukraine&nbsp;
as if it was a boxing match or a TV documentary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:34.480
You've changed your view since then. There's&nbsp;
a lot more than the bare minimum, isn't there?

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:42.880
You know, today when I see the lists of the&nbsp;
weapons which are being delivered by the Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:49.280
allies and when I know for a fact from reports&nbsp;
of the military units that I know personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:54.480
that these weapons get to that, to where&nbsp;
they're supposed to get, and they are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.480 --> 00:18:01.040
used for good use, then I have&nbsp;
no reason to be unhappy about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:07.280
I mean, these weapons and this support, the main&nbsp;
idea is for it to keep coming until the plague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:13.840
of Russia's aggression is stopped and return&nbsp;
back into the within the borders of Russia.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:15.920
You wanted a no fly zone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:22.240
You didn't get it. Do you want NATO's&nbsp;
troops fighting alongside you in Ukraine?

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:26.000
Well, that would be always the preferred option.

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:28.560
But you understand why they're not.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:36.160
At the same time, although that's always the&nbsp;
preferred option, the more we have allied powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:42.640
here side to side with us fighting for freedom&nbsp;
and for democracy and for peace in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:49.680
the better. But at the same time, Ukrainian&nbsp;
army has proven itself to be absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:55.040
self-sustainable when it comes to fighting of the&nbsp;
Russians. As long as we have the means to fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.040 --> 00:19:00.480
with and if the NATO powers and the international&nbsp;
community provides us with those instruments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:06.240
provides us with those weapons and the armaments,&nbsp;
then we're good to go. We can withstand Russia's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:11.280
aggression. We can keep fighting for the rest&nbsp;
of Europe. We can be the protectors of Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:14.320
while protecting ourselves and&nbsp;
our freedom at the same time.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:19.920
But when Putin has openly referred to&nbsp;
his nuclear arsenal and has put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:24.640
he says, on a higher state of alert,&nbsp;
you wouldn't expect the West and NATO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:27.600
in particular not to take that&nbsp;
threat seriously, would you?

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:38.800
In all honesty, Putin has made so many claims&nbsp;
which he never went ahead with, saying that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:45.680
is willing to deploy nuclear weapons in Ukraine&nbsp;
would be suicidal to him and to his own country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:52.240
and his own people as well. And it's a good&nbsp;
thing that it's not just him alone, Putin alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:58.000
who's in charge of pressing that one red button.&nbsp;
There's a whole line of command to start deploying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:04.640
nuclear weapons. And in all honesty, if Putin has&nbsp;
gone completely insane, which is clearly the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:10.320
not all of his generals and not all of the&nbsp;
people in the line of command in Moscow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:15.760
have gone as badly insane as to wish&nbsp;
death upon themselves and their families.

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:21.920
Do you still think that the UN and NATO,&nbsp;
as you said in a tweet on March the 13th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:23.760
are showing weakness and cowardice.

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:36.640
As I said, the UN needs reforming and very fast.&nbsp;
NATO is NATO, I mean, in all fairness, NATO is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:45.680
abiding by the rules in their rulebook. Fair&nbsp;
enough. But at the same time, the different NATO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:52.720
states are showing more, more confidence and more&nbsp;
courage than that whole organisation put together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.520 --> 00:21:00.160
And I think this is the courage that Ukrainians&nbsp;
expect from their partners. Now there's a snap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:05.680
line going around saying be brave like Ukraine&nbsp;
and this is what we want from our friends.

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:10.640
But cowardice is perhaps an&nbsp;
unfair criticism, isn't it?

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:14.560
An unfair criticism of what?

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:16.800
Of NATO and the U.N.?

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:26.240
Well, look, the UN as an organization has deep&nbsp;
structural problems. I've covered already the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:32.720
ground about Russia being a country with a veto&nbsp;
power on the Security Council, being responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:38.640
for the insecurity. The UN could have sent&nbsp;
peacekeeping troops into Ukraine through a General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:45.360
Assembly decision. It hasn't done so for fear of&nbsp;
provoking World War Three. But in all honesty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:52.800
Putin would never dare to attack any other country&nbsp;
for the very reason that he has no resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:58.480
for doing that and for the very reason that&nbsp;
he's concentrated on Ukraine and maybe a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:04.880
of other Eastern European countries, because&nbsp;
Putin, his goal is to rebuild a Russian empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:11.840
This is the ideology behind this war. This is the&nbsp;
ideology behind how Russia functions as a country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:17.840
They want an empire. They feel great in an&nbsp;
empire. They want to bring empires in the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:23.120
possible sense back from the history books. This&nbsp;
is what they are doing. They're not interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:29.920
going ahead with wars in, I don’t know, Asia,&nbsp;
Africa or the Americas because they would not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:37.280
able to pull it off. And when the world stands&nbsp;
united in the same way that Ukrainians stood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:42.000
united against Putin, there would be nothing&nbsp;
that he could do. This war could have stopped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:48.480
Way, way earlier, the three days that the&nbsp;
international experts were giving us as a nation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:53.120
for standing up against the second largest army&nbsp;
in the world could have been really three days to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.120 --> 00:23:01.360
end this war. If NATO forces, UN forces would have&nbsp;
intervened in time this war could have been over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:05.840
in three days, but it could have&nbsp;
been over for Russia in three days.

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:11.600
There are plenty of Western experts who dispute&nbsp;
your assurance that Putin would never press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:16.320
the button and use his nuclear missiles.&nbsp;
But I want, in the time we have left, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.200 --> 00:23:22.160
look at where the war might go now. A month ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:29.360
President Zelenskyy said that negotiation was&nbsp;
the only way out of the conflict. But he added,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:34.400
‘You cannot just demand from Ukraine to recognize&nbsp;
some territories as independent republics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:40.080
These compromises are simply wrong. We have to&nbsp;
come up with a model where Ukraine will not lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:50.160
its sovereignty and its territorial integrity’. Do&nbsp;
you have any idea what that model might look like.

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:56.160
That model is very simple. I mean, Russia's&nbsp;
committing a crime, a crime of aggression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.160 --> 00:24:01.520
There's no doubt about that. I think it's very&nbsp;
much clear for all of the international community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:07.840
what Russia is doing. That crime of aggression&nbsp;
has clear criteria, it’s described in a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:16.320
conventions. And with that, there's also&nbsp;
consequences which pertain to when a crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:21.280
of aggression is committed. And the consequences&nbsp;
are such that, first of all, the crime must be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:27.040
stopped. Hence, Russia must retreat all of its&nbsp;
troops from Ukraine. The borders of Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:32.800
must be reinstated, and then Russia must pay&nbsp;
reparations. It's as simple as that. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:41.360
no other negotiations that can be taking place.&nbsp;
Any negotiations where Ukraine is presumably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:49.120
requested to give up any territories or to make&nbsp;
any kind of other concessions to Russia will fail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:55.360
These negotiations will not bring long-standing&nbsp;
peace to Ukraine, to Europe or to the world. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.080 --> 00:25:03.680
kind of secession negotiations will only incite&nbsp;
more aggression in the world. It will inspire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:09.360
other authoritarian leaders across the&nbsp;
world to follow in Putin's footsteps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:13.600
and they will inspire Putin to go in&nbsp;
for more land grabs. The only solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:21.200
is to make Russia stop its crime of aggression,&nbsp;
to restore the territorial integrity of Ukraine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:26.000
and to make Russia pay for all the crimes&nbsp;
that it has committed in the territory of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:31.120
Ukraine and for the crime of aggression&nbsp;
against international peace and security.

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:35.200
Lesia Vasylenko, it's been very good to have you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:37.920
on Conflict Zone. Thank you&nbsp;
very much indeed. Thank you.

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:41.840
Thank you. It was a pleasure.

